* Straight up nope

 - ruckusrd --get-libdir so userland scripts can use ruckusrd-common.sh w/out
   having to hardcode its intallation path?  like what...?  do we have userland
   scripts that need this?  firstboot_wizard, linuxrc, mount_sysroot,
   mount_sysroot_vfs, system_installer, and umount_sysroot all source it from
   inside the initramfs, but it's guaranteed to live at /ruckusrd-common.sh in
   there.  If we had *real* userland usage, this comment would make sense.  And
   I don't think we'd *want* that file sourced by scripts post-boot anyway.

 - can i have the firstboot wizard ensure network connectivity and than run
   through a domain joining dialog?  domain name, username, password?

   not really...  firstboot runs before NetworkManager, so networking actually
   doesn't come up until later.  i suppose we could store domain, user, and
   password and run something during system bootup?  inject a
   ruckusrd-installer service to do it after Networking is up?  we' would have
   to temporarliy store a password somewhere...  seems a bit wonky, really.

   i'd file this away with non-trivial nice-to-haves, like automatically
   populating awesome-powers/config symlinks based on hostname...

